TEXT: Received 23 May 2026; revised 26 June 2026; accepted 14 August 2026. Date of publication 20 August 2026; date of current version 27 August 2026. Digital Object Identiﬁer 10.1109/OJCOMS.2026.3725365 A Lightweight Language-Model-Driven Agentic Framework for Intrusion Prediction, Detection, and Mitigation in 6G-Enabled IoT Networks ALAEDDINE DIAF 1, ABDELAZIZ AMARA KORBA 1,2 (Senior Member, IEEE), WAEL JAAFAR 3 (Senior Member, IEEE), NOUR ELISLEM KARABADJI 4, AND YACINE GHAMRI-DOUDANE 5 (Senior Member, IEEE) 1Laboratoire Re´ seaux et Syste´ mes (LRS), Badji Mokhtar - Annaba University, Annaba 23000, Algeria 2Computer Science Department, German University of Technology in Oman, Halban Campus, Muscat 130, Oman 3E´ cole de Technologie Supe´ rieure (E´ TS), University of Quebec, Montreal, QC H3C 1K3, Canada 4National Higher School of Technology and Engineering, Laboratoire De Technologies Des Syste` mes E´ nerge´ tiques (LTSE) E3360100, Annaba 23005, Algeria 5L3i, La Rochelle University, 17042 La Rochelle, France CORRESPONDING AUTHOR: W. JAAFAR (wael.jaafar@etsmtl.ca) This work was supported in part by the Mitacs Globalink Research Award (GRA) Program and in part by the Natural Sciences and Engineering Research Council of Canada (NSERC) Discovery under Grant CRSNG RGPIN-2023-03757. ABSTRACT The extensive deployment of Internet of Things (IoT) devices in emerging 6G-enabled environments, characterized by massive connectivity, distributed edge intelligence, and ultra-low-latency communication, allows cyber threats to evade detection until malicious activities manifest. Conventional intrusion detection systems remain fundamentally reactive, identifying threats only after suspicious patterns become observable in network traﬃc, which hinders both proactive threat anticipation and timely mitigation. To address these limitations, this paper proposes a role-based multi-agent cybersecurity framework for 6G-enabled IoT networks that enables proactive intrusion prediction, real-time detection, and knowledge-driven threat mitigation. The framework establishes a coordinated defense loop composed of three specialized agents: (i) a prediction agent that leverages Small Language Models (SLMs) to model packet-level temporal dependencies and proactively forecast malicious traﬃc sequences, (ii) a detection agent that performs real-time traﬃc classiﬁcation using a lightweight gradient boosting model suitable for low-latency edge environments, and (iii) a knowledge graph-driven mitigation agent that maps detected threats to corresponding defensive countermeasures. Extensive evaluation on the CICIoT2023 dataset demonstrates the eﬀectiveness of the proposed framework, achieving 98.24% accuracy in proactive packet-level detection using forecasted packets and 99.94% accuracy in real-time ﬂow-based detection. The proposed framework combines structured multi-agent coordination, low-latency inference, and knowledgedriven response, making it a promising step toward scalable and intelligent cybersecurity management in 6G-enabled IoT networks. INDEX TERMS Security, agentic AI, attack mitigation, autonomous cyber defense, intrusion prediction, LLMs, SLMs, IoT. I. INTRODUCTION T he Internet of Things (IoT) has experienced exponential growth, from consumer applications to critical sectors such as healthcare, defense, and vehicular networks. As IoT evolves toward 6G-enabled networks, massive connectivity, edge intelligence, and ultra-low latency further accelerate this transformation. However, they also broaden the attack surface and shrink the response window [1]. Securing such dynamic environments, therefore, demands cybersecurity mechanisms that are accurate, eﬃcient, adaptive, and capable of coordinated real-time response. Recent reports indicate more than 820,000 daily IoT attack attempts in 2025, along with a 46% increase in malware year-over-year [2]. Massive botnets, such as Aisuru, have launched record-breaking 29.7 Tbps Distributed Denial-ofService (DDoS) attacks, while insecure endpoints contribute to 40% of global DDoS traﬃc [3], [4]. These challenges are further ampliﬁed in dynamic environments such as vehicular 10178 © 2026 The Authors. This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 License. For more information, see https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ VOLUME 7, 2026 networks and emerging 6G-enabled IoT networks, where rapid topological changes and resource constraints render conventional reactive defenses insuﬃcient [5]. Intrusion Detection Systems (IDS) are widely deployed to monitor network activity and identify potential threats in IoT environments. However, most IDS operate reactively at the ﬂow level [6], [7], detecting attacks only after traﬃc aggregation introduces latency. This delay is problematic for fast-evolving threats such as ransomware and advanced persistent threats (APTs) [8]. Packet-level approaches provide ﬁne-grained visibility and enable earlier detection of attack indicators, but face scalability challenges in large-scale deployments [9]. In contrast, ﬂow-level methods improve scalability at the expense of delayed detection [10]. Moreover, most existing IDSs lack integrated mitigation capabilities and remain challenged by resource-constrained IoT environments [11], [12], leaving dynamic networks such as the Internet-of-Vehicles (IoV) particularly vulnerable [13]. The transition toward 6G-enabled IoT networks further ampliﬁes cybersecurity challenges due to ultra-low latency requirements, massive device density, highly dynamic network topologies, and distributed edge computing infrastructures. In such environments, conventional reactive intrusion detection mechanisms become increasingly insuﬃcient, as delayed responses may allow attacks to rapidly propagate across interconnected devices and edge nodes. Consequently, 6G-enabled IoT networks require proactive and autonomous cybersecurity frameworks capable of continuously monitoring traﬃc behavior, anticipating threats before complete attack manifestation, and supporting low-latency adaptive response mechanisms [14]. To address these limitations, recent research [15], [16], [17] has moved toward proactive security approaches that seek to anticipate cyber threats before their occurrence. Although promising, most existing solutions remain limited in scope and primarily focus on isolated prediction tasks, without supporting coordinated decision-making or integrated response mechanisms. In highly dynamic IoT environments, eﬀective defense requires intelligent systems that can continuously monitor network behavior, anticipate emerging threats, and adapt their decisions according to evolving traﬃc patterns [18]. While recent proactive IDS approaches have leveraged machine learning (ML) to anticipate attacks [15], [19], they often struggle to capture long-range temporal dependencies and subtle contextual patterns in network traﬃc. Alternatively, Transformer-based Large Language Models (LLMs), which leverage self-attention mechanisms to process sequential data, oﬀer a promising approach for modeling complex temporal dependencies in network traﬃc. Recent studies [16], [17], [20], [21], [22] have explored their use for proactive intrusion prediction, demonstrating the ability to detect early indicators of malicious activity. However, the high computational and memory requirements of full-scale LLMs limit their applicability in IoT environments. Small Language Models (SLMs) address this challenge by retaining much of the predictive capability of LLMs while being lightweight and resource-eﬃcient [23], [24]. Their compact design enables frequent model updates, low-latency inference, and scalable operation across distributed and resource-constrained networks. Importantly, SLMs provide an eﬃcient foundation for role-specialized cybersecurity systems capable of continuously monitoring network behavior, anticipating threats, and supporting low-latency decisionmaking. These capabilities support emerging role-based multi-agent cybersecurity paradigms that leverage structured coordination and knowledge-guided decision-making to enable proactive and context-aware defense in complex IoT networks [25], [26]. In line with the discussion above, we propose a rolebased multi-agent framework for proactive cyber defense and cooperative response in IoT networks. The proposed framework integrates prediction, detection, and mitigation within a uniﬁed and coordinated network architecture. It leverages SLMs for packet-level intrusion forecasting, a LightGBM-based classiﬁer for real-time detection, and a D3FEND-aligned knowledge graph for knowledge-driven threat mitigation recommendation. The main contributions can be summarized as follows: 1) We design a coordinated multi-agent framework in which prediction, detection, and mitigation agents operate collaboratively, ensuring continuous monitoring, adaptive decision-making, and proactive protection. Speciﬁcally: 2) We propose a proactive intrusion forecasting mechanism that leverages SLMs to predict malicious activity at the packet level, before a network ﬂow is fully completed. 3) We develop a real-time detection system based on a resource-eﬃcient LightGBM classiﬁer. It performs traﬃc classiﬁcation on the ﬂy, oﬀering low latency and scalability for IoT and edge environments. 4) We introduce a knowledge-driven mitigation component that exploits semantic knowledge from the D3FENDTM framework. The system maps detected threats to appropriate countermeasures, supporting structured mitigation recommendation generation. 5) We validate the eﬃcacy of our approach on the large-scale CICIoT2023 dataset. Experimental results demonstrate early threat detection, high classiﬁcation accuracy, and practical feasibility in real-world IoT environments, compared to benchmarks. The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section II overviews the related work, while Section III describes the proposed framework. Section IV presents the experimental setup and datasets, and Section V discusses the results along with a benchmarking against state-of-the-art methods. Finally, Section VI concludes the paper. VOLUME 7, 2026 10179 DIAF et al.: LIGHTWEIGHT LANGUAGE-MODEL-DRIVEN AGENTIC FRAMEWORK II. RELATED WORK This section reviews recent advances in intrusion detection, prediction, and mitigation in IoT networks, with a focus on AI-driven approaches and their limitations in enabling proactive and coordinated cybersecurity. IDSs are necessary to secure IoT networks by monitoring traﬃc and identifying malicious activities. However, the heterogeneous, large-scale, and dynamic nature of IoT environments poses signiﬁcant challenges for traditional IDS, particularly in achieving real-time detection, adapting to evolving threats, and providing integrated mitigation capabilities [27]. The latter have evolved from signature-based detection toward anomaly detection using classical ML algorithms such as Decision Trees, Random Forests, K-Nearest Neighbors, and Logistic Regression [28]. Ensemble models, such as LightGBM, further demonstrated high detection accuracy with low computational overhead in IoT networks [29]. Although they enable eﬃcient traﬃc classiﬁcation, these methods’ ability to capture complex and evolving attack patterns remains limited. Deep learning (DL) models have been increasingly adopted since then to automatically learn discriminative representations from network traﬃc. Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) and Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs), including Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM), capture spatial and temporal patterns, enabling more eﬀective IoT intrusion detection [28], [30], [31]. In parallel, recent work showed that combining ML with feature engineering enhances intrusion detection, in terms of accuracy and false positive events, when using resource-constrained IoT protocols such as MQTT [32]. More recently, hybrid DL architectures and Transformer-based models have been explored to model complex relationships in network traﬃc using attention mechanisms [33]. However, these approaches often require large training datasets and substantial computational resources, limiting their applicability in real-world 6G-enabled IoT networks. Also, most existing IDS remain inherently reactive, detecting malicious activity only after suﬃcient attack patterns have manifested in network traﬃc [34]. Alternatively, generative AI has been explored for cybersecurity in 6G-enabled IoT networks, demonstrating promising intrusion detection capabilities for evolving threat scenarios [35]. Similarly, combined DL with optimization algorithms achieved high detection accuracy while supporting eﬃcient threat detection [36]. Within the last few years, LLM-based hybrid architectures have been proposed. For instance, GPT–BERT–LSTM frameworks [16], LLM agents [37], and BARTPredict [17] have been proposed to forecast traﬃc for early attack detection. Given their computation overhead, SLMs are seen as an interesting alternative for deployment at the edge of the network. For example, lightweight BERT variants (DistilBERT, MobileBERT, and TinyBERT) enabled eﬃcient attack detection inference with reduced resource requirements [38], [39], [40]. Moreover, SLMs are increasingly recognized as a promising component of agentic AI [25], highlighting their potential role in coordinated and knowledge-driven cybersecurity systems. While predictive models provide foresight into potential attacks, eﬀective cybersecurity in 6G-enabled IoT environments requires coordinated mechanisms for real-time response and mitigation support. In fact, traditional mitigation strategies, based on rule-based ﬁrewalls and static segmentation, struggle to adapt to evolving threats and the dynamic nature of IoT networks [41]. In parallel, reinforcement learning (RL) has been explored for adaptive mitigation, where agents learn optimal defense policies through simulated interactions [42], [43]. Multi-agent RL (MARL) frameworks have further enabled coordinated distributed responses across IoT nodes, demonstrating resilience against various attack scenarios [44], [45], [46]. Nevertheless, these approaches were computationally intensive and often exhibited limited eﬃciency–performance trade-oﬀs. Existing studies mainly focused on isolated aspects of cybersecurity in 6G-enabled IoT networks, such as traﬃc prediction, intrusion detection, or knowledge-driven mitigation, often treating these tasks independently. However, recent studies emphasized that proactive threat identiﬁcation, adaptive mitigation strategies, and self-sustaining network operation are key requirements for future end-to-end 6G network automation [14]. In this context, the proposed framework integrates packet-level traﬃc prediction, ﬂow-level intrusion detection, and mitigation recommendation generation within a coordinated multi-agent architecture. Moreover, unlike computationally intensive RL- and MARL-based defense approaches [47], [48], our framework leverages SLM-driven prediction and knowledge-guided mitigation mechanisms to enable proactive cybersecurity while maintaining computational eﬃciency for large-scale IoT environments. III. PROPOSED FRAMEWORK This section presents our multi-agent framework, which comprises three complementary autonomous agents that collaboratively perform packet forecasting, intrusion detection, and knowledge-driven mitigation. These agents interact through an agent coordination layer responsible for task orchestration, information exchange, and contextual preservation across the prediction–detection–mitigation cycle. Fig. 1 illustrates the overall architecture and inter-agent data ﬂows. A. FRAMEWORK OVERVIEW AND DATA REPRESENTATION The proposed architecture is structured around three specialized agents: a Prediction Agent, a Detection Agent, and a Mitigation Agent. Through the coordination layer, they operate as a uniﬁed system that enables continuous decision ﬂow and adaptive feedback. As illustrated in Fig. 1, data ﬂows sequentially from the Prediction Agent to the Detection Agent and then to the Mitigation Agent, with feedback loops supporting coordinated decision propagation across the framework. Table 1 complements this architectural view by 10180 VOLUME 7, 2026 FIGURE 1. Pipeline of the proposed multi-agent framework. The Prediction Agent forecasts future packets using SLMs, the Detection Agent combines packet-level and ﬂow-level analysis for proactive intrusion detection, and the Mitigation Agent maps detected attacks to D3FEND-aligned countermeasures through coordinated agent interaction. TABLE 1. Summary of Agent Roles, Inputs, Outputs, and Key Tasks in the Proposed Framework. summarizing the inputs, outputs, primary roles, and key tasks of each agent and the coordination layer. At a high level, the framework operates in three sequential stages. First, the Prediction Agent observes a history of packets belonging to a given network ﬂow and forecasts the next packet, while validating its plausibility against prior context to ﬁlter inconsistent predictions. Second, the Detection Agent processes both the forecasted packet and real-time ﬂow data, combining ﬂow-level classiﬁcation for high-conﬁdence detection with packet-level analysis enriched by ﬂow context for early threat identiﬁcation. Third, upon detecting malicious activity, the Mitigation Agent maps the attack category to MITRE ATT&CK techniques and retrieves appropriate countermeasures from the D3FEND knowledge graph. This design yields a uniﬁed, lightweight, and proactive defense strategy suitable for dynamic and resource-constrained 6G-enabled IoT environments. To support agent reasoning, network traﬃc is represented through structured packet-level and ﬂow-level datasets that preserve temporal dependencies and protocol semantics, serving as a common input space for both prediction and detection. 1) PACKET-LEVEL REPRESENTATION FOR PREDICTIONS IoT network communications are modeled as ordered packet sequences associated with individual traﬃc ﬂows. Instead VOLUME 7, 2026 10181 DIAF et al.: LIGHTWEIGHT LANGUAGE-MODEL-DRIVEN AGENTIC FRAMEWORK detection on forecasted packets. Each instance combines packet-level features with rolling ﬂow statistics computed only from past packets, ensuring temporal causality. - D2C: a ﬂow-classiﬁcation dataset used for real-time intrusion detection. Each ﬂow is summarized by 65 aggregated features capturing temporal statistics (e.g., inter-arrival times, packet size variations), TCP characteristics (window sizes, roundtrip-time (RTT), sequence/acknowledgment behavior), and IP/ICMP metrics (time-to-live (TTL), options, echo ratios). These representations are designed to maintain low computational complexity while retaining suﬃcient discriminative power for high-accuracy detection. FIGURE 2. Textual packet-ﬂow representation used in the DF forecasting dataset. Each packet is transformed into a sequence of feature tokens separated by [SEP] mark [...] ext achieves an inference latency of 0.144 ms per packet and a throughput of 6,941 packets/s. Despite the increased feature dimensionality, the model maintains a compact storage footprint of 17.9 MB while preserving submillisecond inference performance. The reported latency and throughput measurements correspond exclusively to the packet-level and ﬂow-level detection modules, including feature extraction, feature processing, and classiﬁcation with the trained LightGBM models. They do VOLUME 7, 2026 10189 DIAF et al.: LIGHTWEIGHT LANGUAGE-MODEL-DRIVEN AGENTIC FRAMEWORK TABLE 11. Attack Mitigation Mapping Based on D3FEND Strategies. not include the execution time associated with SLM-based packet forecasting, DistilBERT validation, inter-agent coordination, or D3FEND knowledge graph retrieval. Therefore, the reported values should be interpreted as the computational cost of the detection components rather than the complete end-to-end multi-agent framework. Extending the analysis to the full framework remains an important direction for future work. C. MITIGATION ANALYSIS In this work, the evaluation of attack-to-countermeasure mappings aims to validate the correctness of the proposed mitigation reasoning process rather than executing or benchmarking live mitigation actions. Speciﬁcally, we assess whether each detected attack category is properly mapped to standardized ATT&CK techniques and subsequently aligned with appropriate D3FEND-based defensive strategies. This evaluation is performed through structured analysis of the generated mappings with respect to established cybersecurity knowledge bases. Hence, the evaluation is knowledge-driven and emphasizes the quality of mitigation recommendations rather than operational performance metrics such as latency or deployment impact. Accordingly, the reported results assess the correctness and relevance of the generated recommendations rather than the eﬀectiveness of their real-world execution. Table 11 summarizes the mapping between detected attack categories, corresponding ATT&CK techniques, and D3FEND-aligned mitigation strategies. The results demonstrate that the proposed “Mitigation Agent” consistently translates detection outputs into structured defense recommendations using standardized cybersecurity knowledge. To illustrate the complete mitigation reasoning process, consider a detected DDoS-HTTP Flood attack. The Mitigation Agent ﬁrst maps this alert to the ATT&CK technique T1498.002 (Network Denial of Service). Using this technique as input, the D3FEND knowledge graph identiﬁes the corresponding defensive strategies, namely Evict, Harden, and Isolate. These strategies are then translated into concrete recommended actions such as rate limiting, deployment of a Web Application Firewall (WAF), and traﬃc ﬁltering. This example demonstrates the complete reasoning chain from detected attack class to ATT&CK technique, D3FEND strategy selection, and actionable mitigation recommendation. The distribution of mitigation strategies across attack categories is illustrated in Fig. 6. It highlights how diﬀerent FIGURE 6. Distribution of D3FEND-aligned mitigation strategies across different attack categories, illustrating how the Mitigation Agent maps detected attacks to defense actions using ATT&CK-to-D3FEND reasoning. classes of attacks activate distinct defensive behaviors within the proposed framework. In particular, volumetric attacks (DDoS and DoS) are predominantly mapped to Evict and Isolate strategies, reﬂecting their eﬀectiveness in mitigating resource exhaustion through connection reset, traﬃc ﬁltering, and rate limiting. Reconnaissance activities are associated with Detect and Harden strategies, emphasizing monitoring and access control. Spooﬁng attacks exhibit a more diverse mitigation proﬁle, combining Harden, Isolate, and Deceive strategies, reﬂecting the need for layered defenses such as ARP inspection, packet ﬁltering, and DNS traﬃc redirection. D. COMPARATIVE ANALYSIS AND KEY INSIGHTS Table 12 compares the proposed framework with recent agentic, traditional, and proactive intrusion detection approaches across multiple criteria, including architecture, model scale, detection granularity, prediction capability, mitigation support, and accuracy. Recent agentic frameworks such as AgenticCyber [58] and the LLM-based multi-agent system for LAE-IoT [59] (>90%) demonstrate strong detection performance with accuracies 96.8% and over 90%, respectively. However, they remain primarily reactive, relying on ﬂow-level or multimodal data without incorporating proactive prediction. Their dependence on LLMs also introduces signiﬁcant computational overhead, limiting their suitability for resourceconstrained IoT environments. The single-agent IDS [57] achieves a lower accuracy of 79.5%, lacking the multiagent coordination and proactive capabilities. Traditional DL 10190 VOLUME 7, 2026 TABLE 12. Comparative Analysis of Agentic, Traditional and Proactive IDS Approaches. approaches evaluated on IoT datasets [31] and ensemble models such as LightGBM [29] provide eﬃcient ﬂowlevel detection of 96.6% and 99.41%, respectively, yet they remain limited to reactive analysis without forecasting or response mechanisms. Moreover, proactive predictionbased works [16], [17] and multi-agent DDoS defense frameworks [46] demonstrate the feasibility of anticipating or early detecting malicious activity with high accuracy (over 98%), but typically operate without fully integrating detection and mitigation within a uniﬁed pipeline or exploiting multi-granularity packet–ﬂow analysis. In contrast, the proposed framework combines a multi-agent architecture with SLM-based forecasting (99.90% validation rate), hybrid packet-ﬂow detection, and knowledge-driven mitigation. It achieves 98.24% accuracy for proactive detection on forecasted packets and 99.94% for real-time ﬂow-based detection on the CICIoT2023 dataset, thus consistently outperforming existing methods. This comparison highlights the fact that existing methods address individual aspects of intrusion detection, i.e., detection and/or prediction and/or response, whereas the proposed framework integrates these components into a coherent and autonomous pipeline. This integration, combined with multigranularity analysis and lightweight modeling, results in a scalable, interpretable, and end-to-end eﬃcient cybersecurity defense system. VI. CONCLUSION This paper introduced a multi-agent cybersecurity framework for 6G-enabled IoT networks that integrates SLM-based packet forecasting, multi-granularity LightGBM detection at both packet and ﬂow levels, and D3FEND-aligned mitigation within a coordinated architecture. Experiments on the CICIoT2023 dataset showed that the framework achieves 98.24% accuracy in proactive intrusion detection on forecasted packets and 99.94% accuracy in real-time ﬂow-based detection. By unifying prediction, detection, and mitigationrecommendation generation into a single pipeline, our framework demonstrated the promise of lightweight agentic architectures for proactive and adaptive IoT cybersecurity. Despite these strengths, the evaluation relied on a single IoT dataset, which, although recent and comprehensive, may not fully represent the heterogeneity of real-world 6G-enabled IoT deployments or the breadth of emerging attack vectors. Moreover, the mitigation module currently depends on a static D3FEND-based knowledge graph, lim- iting adaptability to novel or evolving threats, and produces structured recommendations rather than autonomously exe- cuted responses. These constraints highlight the need for broader validation and more dynamic mitigation capabilities. Consequently, future work will focus on enabling auto- mated mitigation with veriﬁcation mechanisms supported by SLMs, expanding evaluation across multiple IoT datasets to assess generalization, and incorporating continuously updated cybersecurity knowledge to enhance adaptability against emerging threats.
